*Reference: [CLI](cli.tmd) · [Shell completion](shell-completion.tmd) · [Front matter](frontmatter.tmd) · Cell options · [Configuration](configuration.tmd) · [Shortcodes](shortcodes.tmd) · [Troubleshooting](troubleshooting.tmd)*

A code cell carries per-cell options on its leading comment lines. These tune
whether the source shows, whether the cell runs, whether its output is cached, and
how the output is captioned, numbered, and cross-referenced. This page is the
complete list. For the narrative on writing and running cells, see
[Executable content](../using/code.tmd); for the document-wide `execute:` defaults
that cell options override, see the
[Configuration reference](configuration.tmd).

## How options are written

An option is a `key: value` line at the **top** of the cell, behind a directive
prefix. By convention the prefix is the cell's own comment marker plus a pipe:

| Cell language | Prefix | Example |
|---|---|---|
| `{python}`, `{r}` | `#\|` | `#\| echo: false` |
| `{js}` | `//\|` | `//\| viewof: harmonics` |
| `{mermaid}` | `%%\|` | `%%\| label: fig-flow` |

Picking the cell's own comment marker keeps the option line a valid comment in that
language. All three prefixes (`#\|`, `//\|`, `%%\|`) are accepted regardless of the
cell's language, so the table shows the idiomatic choice rather than an enforced one.
A space between the comment marker and the pipe is tolerated (`# | echo: false`,
`// | name: x`), but the canonical form is tight.

### The leading-block rule

Options are read **only from the contiguous run of directive lines at the very top
of the cell.** The first line that is not an option directive stops option parsing,
and everything from there on is treated as code. So this works:

```python
#| echo: false
#| label: fig-loss
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
```

But here the `label` is invisible to Taliesin, because a blank line (and then real
code) already ended the option block:

```python
#| echo: false

#| label: fig-loss
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
```

Keep every option line flush against the top of the cell with no blank line
between them. A directive that appears *after* code is just an ordinary comment.

### Unknown keys are caught

The option vocabulary is closed. A key Taliesin does not recognize (a typo, or an
option it does not implement) is reported as a **located warning** with a
"did you mean" hint when a known key is within edit distance 2:

```text
slides.tmd:14: unknown cell option `labl` (did you mean `label`?)
```

The cell still renders exactly as before, the warning just points at the line. In
live preview you can click it straight to the source. `taliesin check` surfaces the
same warning as a publish gate. (Front-matter and `_site.yml` keys are validated
the same way; see the [Configuration reference](configuration.tmd).)

## Every option

An option marked `(none)` in the **Default** column does nothing unless you set it.

| Option | Languages | Default | Effect |
|---|---|---|---|
| `echo` | `python`, `r` | `true` | `false` hides the source listing; the cell still runs and its output still shows |
| `include` | `python`, `r` | `true` | `false` hides **both** source and output; the cell still runs (kernel state is kept) |
| `cache` | `python`, `r` | `true` | `false` opts the cell out of the `_freeze/` cache, so it always re-executes |
| `label` | `python`, `r`, `mermaid`, `js` | (none) | `fig-X` / `lst-X` / `tbl-X` makes the cell a numbered, cross-referenceable figure / listing / table (resolve with `@fig-X` etc.) |
| `fig-cap` | `python`, `r`, `mermaid`, `js` | (none) | A caption for the output; also turns the output into a numbered `<figure>` |
| `lst-cap` | `python`, `r` | (none) | A caption for the **source listing**, making it a numbered listing |
| `tbl-cap` | `python`, `r` | (none) | A caption for the output **table**, making it a numbered table |
| `fig-export` | `python`, `r` | (none) | Comma-separated paths; a `python` cell's figures are auto-written print-clean to disk, while an `r` cell must write its own file (e.g. `ggsave()`). Folded into the cache key either way, so changing a path re-runs the cell |
| `code-fold` | `python`, `r` | (none) | `true` collapses the listing into a closed `<details>`; `show` folds it but starts open |
| `code-summary` | `python`, `r` | `"Code"` | The summary label for a `code-fold` listing |
| `code-line-numbers` | `python`, `r` | `false` | Wraps each source line so a deck can step through / highlight `\|`-separated line ranges (e.g. `"1\|3-5"`); there is no line-number gutter |
| `name` | `js` | (none) | Publish the cell's return value into the reactive scope under this name (a helper other `{js}` cells read) |
| `viewof` | `js` | (none) | The cell returns a DOM input; its value is registered under this name for other cells to consume |
| `input` | `js` | (none) | Comma-separated names; re-run this cell when any named input, `viewof`, or Python `define` value changes |

Anything not in this list warns. `fig-cap` / `tbl-cap` / `lst-cap` each *imply* a
number even without a `label`: the caption alone is enough to make a numbered
figure, table, or listing. Add a `label` only when you want to cross-reference it.

::: {.callout-note}
## A label decides the kind

A `label:` selects which numbered thing the cell becomes. `fig-`, `lst-`, and
`tbl-` prefixes route to figure, listing, and table respectively, so a labelled
cell's caption shows up in the matching numbering and `@fig-`/`@lst-`/`@tbl-`
references resolve to it. A bare `fig-cap` with no label still numbers the figure,
it just can't be referenced.
:::

## echo vs include vs cache

These three are the ones worth getting right, because they look similar and do very
different things. All three are about *execution behaviour*, and they are
independent:

| Option set | Does the cell run? | Source shown? | Output shown? | Cached? |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| (defaults) | yes | yes | yes | yes |
| `echo: false` | yes | **no** | yes | yes |
| `include: false` | yes | no | **no** | yes |
| `cache: false` | yes | yes | yes | **no** |

The crucial distinctions:

- **`include: false` still runs the cell.** It only drops the *output* from the
  page; the code executes against the warm kernel and any variables, imports, or
  side effects persist for the cells that follow. This is the option for a setup
  cell whose result you never want to see but whose state you need downstream. It is
  not a way to skip a cell. (To skip execution entirely, run with `--no-exec` /
  `TALIESIN_NO_EXEC`, which applies to the whole document.)
- **`echo: false` hides the source only.** The output is still rendered, the cell
  still runs. Use it when the reader should see a plot or a table but not the code
  that made it.
- **`cache: false` always re-executes.** The cell never restores from the
  `_freeze/` cache and its output is never written there, and everything downstream
  of it re-runs too. Reach for it whenever a cell's result depends on something
  that is not the cell's own code: a data file it reads, an environment variable, a
  network resource, the clock, or a sample without a fixed seed. Everything else
  caches by default, keyed by a cumulative content hash, so an unchanged cell
  replays instead of re-running.

```python
#| include: false
#| cache: false
# Runs for its side effects; nothing appears on the page.
# `cache: false` because the result depends on data.csv, which the cache key
# cannot see (see the note below).
import pandas as pd
df = pd.read_csv("data.csv")
```

```python
#| echo: false
# The code is hidden, the chart it draws is shown.
df.plot()
```

```python
#| cache: false
# Always re-runs; never frozen.
import datetime
print(datetime.datetime.now())
```

::: {.callout-important}
## The cache key sees your code, not your data

The `_freeze/` key is built from cell code, every upstream cell's code, and the
interpreter's identity. That makes a stale hit impossible when you edit a cell or
anything above it. It also means the key is blind to everything a cell reads that
*is not* code: a CSV or Parquet file, an environment variable, a config file, a
URL it fetches, the clock, an installed library you upgraded in place.

Edit `data.csv` without touching a line of Python and the numbers on the page will
not change, because as far as the cache is concerned nothing happened. Mark any
cell with an out-of-band input `#| cache: false`, as in the example above.

To force one fresh run without editing anything, use the dev-menu **Restart
kernel** or run with `TALIESIN_NO_CACHE`.
:::

::: {.callout-tip}
## `include: false` keeps state; it does not skip the cell

If you want a cell's variables available downstream but want nothing on the page,
`include: false` is the answer. If you instead expect `include: false` to make a
cell *not run* (to save time, say), it will surprise you: the cell still executes.
There is no per-cell "don't run" option, because the cells form a single
sequential program against one kernel, and skipping one mid-stream would leave the
ones after it referring to undefined state.
:::

## Document-level defaults

The front-matter `execute:` block sets document-wide defaults for `echo`,
`include`, and `cache`. Each defaults to `true`, and a per-cell `#|` option always
wins:

```yaml
---
title: "Report"
execute:
  echo: false      # hide all source by default
  cache: true      # (the default; shown for completeness)
---
```

With `execute: echo: false` set, every cell hides its source unless a cell opts
back in with its own `#| echo: true`. The override is per-key: a cell that sets
`#| echo: true` still inherits the document's `include` and `cache` defaults. Only
`echo`, `include`, and `cache` live under `execute:`; the `eval` / `output` /
`warning` keys some other tools put there are not honoured. See the
[Configuration reference](configuration.tmd) for where `execute:` sits in front
matter.

## `{js}` reactive options

The three `{js}`-only options wire a cell into the in-browser reactive graph
instead of the kernel. They run client-side, so `echo` / `include` / `cache` (which
are about the Python/R kernel) are not honoured on a `{js}` cell. A `{js}` cell
renders only its output container; its source is never shown as a listing, so there
is nothing for `echo` to hide.

- `//| viewof: NAME` makes the cell return a DOM control and registers its value
  under `NAME`. Other cells read it with `qmd.value("NAME")`.
- `//| name: NAME` publishes the cell's return value under `NAME` for other cells.
  Other cells read it with `qmd.get("NAME")`.
- `//| input: A, B` re-runs the cell whenever input `A`, input `B`, or a matching
  Python `define` value changes.

The two readers are not interchangeable. `qmd.value("NAME")` reads an input value: a
`viewof`, an `{{< input >}}` control, or a `define` value. `qmd.get("NAME")` reads
a value published by another cell's `//| name:`.

A `//| input:` that names something no cell, `viewof`, or `{{< input >}}` control
defines is flagged by `check` as a dangling reactive input, and a cycle among
`{js}` cells (each consuming the other) is flagged too. The dangling-input check is
suppressed when the document contains any Python or R cell, since a runtime
`define` call could publish the name; the cycle check always runs. See
[Executable content](../using/code.tmd) for the full reactive walkthrough.
